fact WorkItem(id: "WI-1017", description: some(value: "Occurs-check the non-Term result bindings that finish_result_value now produces. The WI-649 note at resolve.rs:1102-1112 says the non-Term bind_waking path is 'NOT occurs-checked ... safe today only by ABSENCE OF A PRODUCER: every builtin that writes a non-Term extra binding is either occurs-checked already (builtin_unify) or has no live anthill caller ... If a reflect op that quotes an open term over a shared var ever gains a caller, this becomes the same cyclic-sigma route WI-649 closed at the fact fast-path.' WI-1015 created exactly that producer: finish_result_value binds a builtin result as a Value, and field_access (the desugaring of every ?x.y) plus resolve_sort_instantiation_param now hand a Value::Node / Value::Entity CHILD OF THE GOAL back through extra.bind_value. Concretely field_access(?obj, 'f', ?r) with ?obj sigma-bound to an occurrence Point(f: g(?r)) binds ?r -> Value::Node(g(?r)) with no occurs-check, and reify_value / substitute_occurrence then have no terminating walk over it. Found by /code-review on WI-1015; NOT fixed there because occurs-checking the value-binding path is a design change in WI-649's territory, not an arm. Scope: decide whether finish_result_value occurs-checks (bind_value_unifying already routes through the occurs-checked unify_match_values, so the machinery exists), or whether the projection path must refuse a result that shares a var with its receiver; then update the WI-649 note, which currently states the producer does not exist. Drive it with a rule that projects a field mentioning the result var - the pre-fix behaviour should be a hang or a cyclic binding, not a wrong answer."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-08-05T12:46:35Z"))

fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-1017", author: "user", content: "CORRECTION to this item's own premise, then FIXED. This item said 'WI-1015 created exactly that producer'. It did not. MEASURED by probe on the PRE-WI-1015 path — a hash-consed Term receiver producing a Value::Term binding, no new carrier involved: field_access(ProbePoint(x: probe_g(?r)), 'x', ?r) returned ONE solution, bound ?r to TermId(probe_g(?r)) with is_contradiction()==false, and kb.reify of that answer OVERFLOWED THE STACK and aborted the process. So the hole was reachable from ordinary dot projection before WI-1015; the non-Term producers it added only widened the carrier set. The WI-649 note's framing is what hid it: it reasoned about 'non-Term extra bindings' and concluded absence-of-a-producer, but the merge loop never distinguished carriers, and field_access — the desugaring of every ?x.y — has had a live caller all along. FIX: the occurs_in_value guard the note itself prescribed, in the SuccessWithBindings merge loop, checked against new_subst as it accumulates so a cycle formed across two bindings of one extra is caught too; a positive check drops the branch exactly as BuiltinResult::Failure does. Pinned by a_projection_binding_its_own_receiver_var_is_refused, whose control is the stack-overflow abort above and which also asserts an ACYCLIC projection still binds, so the guard refuses cycles and not projections. Full workspace green, 4149 passed; suite runtime unchanged (259s vs 260s), so the per-binding check costs nothing measurable.", at: "2026-08-05T12:46:31Z")
